<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cecil Rhodes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a>–<a href="/years/1902">1902</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cecil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> was a British businessman and politician in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> who made a fortune in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> diamonds through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-beers">De Beers</a> and in gold. Prime Minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> from 1890 to 1896, he used his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashonaland">Mashonaland</a> in 1890 and conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> kingdom in 1893, lands named Rhodesia after him. His part in the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a> of 1895 forced his resignation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes.jpg" alt="Cecil Rhodes" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bassano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cecil_John_Rhodes_(Cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cecil John Rhodes was born on 5 July 1853 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>'s Stortford in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>, the fourth son and sixth child of the Reverend Francis William Rhodes, vicar of the town. His family sent him to Natal as a teenager in the hope that the climate would improve his poor health, and he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> on 1 September 1870. He worked on his brother's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> farm, and in 1871 entered the diamond trade at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a>. In 1873 he entered Oriel College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, but stayed one term before illness sent him back to Kimberley, and he returned to Oxford in 1876. His admiration for the Oxford tutorial system later shaped his plans for a scholarship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With funding from Rothschild and Company, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> began to buy out and consolidate diamond mines, and gained a near-complete monopoly of the world diamond market. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-beers">De Beers</a> in 1888. He entered the Cape Parliament in 1881 and became prime minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in 1890. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glen-grey-act">Glen Grey Act</a> he took land from black Africans, saying that they had to be driven off their land to stimulate them to labour, and the Franchise and Ballot Act tripled the wealth requirement for voting, which in effect barred black voters.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> used his wealth and that of his partner Alfred Beit to obtain mineral concessions from indigenous chiefs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudd-concession">Rudd Concession</a>, signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobengula">Lobengula</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a>, left out the promise that no more than ten white men would mine in Matabeleland, and when Lobengula tried to renounce it the British government ignored him. Settlers and the company's police committed widespread rape of Ndebele women in the early 1890s. The company named Rhodesia after him in 1895. He was forced to resign as prime minister in 1896 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a>, an unauthorised attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-kruger">Paul Kruger</a>'s South African Republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> died of heart failure on 26 March 1902, aged 48, at his cottage in Muizenberg. At his request he was buried at Malindidzimu in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matopos-hills">Matopos</a> hills, a site of great importance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> traditional religion, a choice interpreted as a gesture of colonial conquest. His will founded the Rhodes Scholarship at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, which his estate funds, with the aim of promoting leadership and of rendering war impossible through friendship between the great powers. Critics cite his seizure of land and his promotion of false claims that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> was built by European civilisations, and some historians attack him as a ruthless imperialist and white supremacist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cecil Rhodes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…protection, encouraged by the example of Khama III of the Bangwato in Bechuanaland, opened the lands north of the Zambezi to the British South Africa Company of <b>Cecil Rhodes</b>, and the concessions he signed became the legal basis of company claims over much of what became North-Western Rhodesia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cecil Rhodes</b>, prime minister from 1890 to 1896, raised the property qualification for voters through the Franchise and Ballot Act of 1892 and introduced the Glen Grey Act of 1894, which created individual land tenure and local councils in African areas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Katanga, contested with <b>Cecil Rhodes</b>’s British South Africa Company, an expedition under William Stairs reached the Yeke capital in December 1891, and the Yeke king Msiri was shot dead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Zimbabwe Bird on the national flag copies soapstone carvings from the ruins; one was taken by a hunter in 1889 and sold to <b>Cecil Rhodes</b>, and four were returned from South Africa in 1981.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Kingdom of Zimbabwe · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The territory came under British control through <b>Cecil Rhodes</b> and his British South Africa Company.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The conflict between Kruger’s government, the mining magnates, <b>Cecil Rhodes</b> and the British High Commissioner Alfred Milner drew the great powers into the region, and the telegram of congratulation that Kaiser Wilhelm II sent Kruger after the Jameson Raid strained Anglo-German relations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bishop's Stortford, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Muizenberg, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.87,0.17&amp;year=1884" aria-label="Bishop's Stortford on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4554 370 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cecil Rhodes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5004 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5004 640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.87,0.17&amp;year=1884">What was here in 1884? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
